# Part Three — The Examiner's Record of Topic 6

## Every question, 2016–2026

@py topic_questions 6

## What the record reveals

- **Eight full questions and one short note** (==8.5 visits==), and **14 of the 140 MCQs** — mostly firsts and numbers.
- **Quotas are the favourite line:** ==2016== (pros and cons), ==2021== (is it showing results?), ==2024== (impact of the quota system) and ==2025== (history and evolution).
- **Participation follows:** ==2017== (stabilise democracy, boost development), ==2018== (equal numbers — "half the sky") and ==2024== (a human right?).
- **Representatives** (==2022==) and **leadership** (==2020==) have been asked once each.
- **Suffrage** was a short note (==2019==) and an MCQ theme (2019, 2024).
- **Never yet asked in full:** defining governance; women as **voters** (the registration and turnout gap); the **South Asian case studies**; the glass cliff and women in the bureaucracy and judiciary.

## Five likely questions

1. "Discuss the gender gap in voter registration and turnout in Pakistan. What measures have been taken, and what more is needed?" *(Chapter 3.)*
2. "Compare women's political representation in Pakistan with that in Nepal, India and Bangladesh. What lessons can Pakistan learn?" *(Chapter 10.)*
3. "Define governance. Why is gender equality essential for good governance?" *(Chapter 1.)*
4. "Should Pakistan's reserved seats for women be filled through direct election? Discuss." *(Chapters 5–7; Debate 1.)*
5. "Critically examine the role of the suffrage movement in shaping women's political rights in the West and South Asia." *(Chapter 2.)*

These are not solved here; the Prediction Papers include answers to predicted questions. Plan each as an answer map for practice.
